            <section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Experimental Research · Thermal Analysis · Python</p><h1>Thermal Diffusion in Digital Composites</h1><p class="subtitle">Graduate Research in the Ni Lab</p><p class="hero-intro">Investigating transient heat transfer in architected liquid-metal composite structures, with current work focused on characterizing their electrical-to-thermal response and surface-temperature evolution.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Duke University</span><span>Jan. 2026 – Present</span><span>Graduate researcher</span></div><p class="status">Ongoing research · Experimental approach in development</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#current-study">Current study ↓</a><a class="button" href="#earlier-work">Earlier motion-analysis work ↓</a></div></div><aside class="focus-panel" aria-label="Current research focus"><p class="eyebrow">Current Focus</p><h2>Surface Temperature Over Time</h2><p>How does the architecture of a digital-composite element influence the surface response during electrical heating?</p></aside></section>
            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#current-study">Current study</a><a href="#contributions">My work</a><a href="#earlier-work">Earlier research</a></nav>
            <section class="section split" id="current-study"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>Characterize the Thermal Response</h2></div><div class="copy"><p class="lead">Connect electrical heating inside a composite element to the temperature observed at its surface.</p><p>My current research examines thermal diffusion and transient heat transfer in architected digital composites. The study focuses on how material properties and spatial architecture influence heat propagation and surface-temperature evolution.</p><p>I am developing an experimental approach for a liquid-metal digital-composite element, building on the lab's earlier work on its electrical-to-thermal response. The current focus is characterizing the surface response over time.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Study status</strong><p>The experimental approach is in development. Surface-temperature characterization is the current research direction.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Research Work</h2></div><p>My work combines experimental development with Python-based analysis.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Developing an approach to characterize the electrical-to-thermal response of liquid-metal digital-composite elements and their transient surface-temperature behavior.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Using an experimental research background in setup, calibration, and time-dependent data analysis to support the thermal study.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Previously developed Python video-analysis workflows for soft robotic fish-tail research, including motion tracking and flow characterization.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section" id="earlier-work"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Earlier Motion and Flow Analysis</h2></div><p>Before shifting to the thermal study, I supported the lab's soft robotic fish-tail research through experimental and computer-vision analysis.</p></div><div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Stereo tracking</h3><p>Developed camera-calibration and tracking workflows to analyze motion from synchronized video recordings.</p></article><article><h3>Flow characterization</h3><p>Worked with Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV) methods to support analysis of motion and surrounding flow.</p></article><article><h3>Python analysis</h3><p>Processed experimental video and time-series data to examine time-dependent and frequency-domain behavior.</p></article></div><p class="credits">Ni Lab, Duke University. Research adviser: Dr. Ni.</p></section>

